`CosmosClient` must have the SDK's serializer options set.  Undetermined if there is a way to automatically handle this.  For now, the following must be used when using a custom `CosmosClient`.

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton(sp =>
{
    // CosmosClient needs the SDK's serializer options
    var clientOptions = new CosmosClientOptions()
    {
        UseSystemTextJsonSerializerWithOptions = ProtocolJsonSerializer.SerializationOptions
    };

    return new CosmosClient(
        "https://myCosmosDbEndpoint/",
        "mySuperSecretKey",
        clientOptions);
});

builder.Services.AddSingleton<IStorage>(sp =>
{
    var client = sp.GetRequiredService<CosmosClient>();
    var dbOptions = new CosmosDbPartitionedStorageOptions()
    {
        DatabaseId = "agent",
        ContainerId = "agent-storage"
    };

    return new CosmosDbPartitionedStorage(client, dbOptions);
});
```

- Re-adds the poolManager address as a construction parameter to `BaseHook`, since even trough the PoolManager is a Singleton contract, it has a different address across different chains.
- Updates tests.
- In order to allow for the proper combination of hooks via inheritance, and avoiding the [inheritance diamond problem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiple_inheritance), all hooks have been made `abstract`, and the common parent (BaseHook) construction should only happen at the `final` children. 

i.e.

```
contract BaseHook {
    constructor(address _poolManager) {
        poolManager = _poolManager;
    }
}

abstract contract BaseHookFee is BaseHook {...}

abstract contract BaseAsyncSwap is BaseHook {...}

contract MyCombinedHook is BaseHookFee, BaseAsyncSwap {
    constructor(address _poolManager) BaseHook(_poolManager) {}
}

```

- Adds Mocks for hooks that were not abstract before.

